# Related Work & Positioning — RankCRC

> Current positioning for the RankCRC headline (replaces the stale CU-GR `related_positioning.md`, kept for history). Locked / experiment-independent. The contribution is **distribution-free risk-controlled selective reliability** for recommendation, not raw-ranking SOTA.

## Conformal prediction and conformal risk control
Split conformal prediction \citep{vovk2005algorithmic,angelopoulos2023gentle} gives distribution-free coverage for set-valued predictors; Conformal Risk Control \citep{angelopoulos2024crc} extends this to any bounded, monotone loss, yielding `E[L(λ̂)] ≤ α` in marginal expectation. RCPS \citep{bates2021rcps} and Learn-then-Test \citep{angelopoulos2021ltt} give the high-probability (PAC) analogue via multiple-testing. **RankCRC's difference:** the controlled object is **listwise rank-risk** (`1 − NDCG@k`) on the **served slice after abstention** (a self-normalized conditional mean), not a pointwise loss over the full population. We show plain CRC does not attach to the naive served-slice estimator (random-denominator winner's curse) and repair it with a frozen-`λ` B1/B2 split so the served mean is an honest i.i.d. mean; we name LTT as the PAC upgrade and a numerator+coverage-floor route as an exact surrogate. Conceptually, the novel object is **risk-controlled reliability of ranking quality on a self-selected served slice** — a guarantee on a conditional mean over the users a label-free policy chooses to serve, rather than a marginal full-population loss. (We phrase the precedence claim narrowly and only "to our knowledge," and we do not lean on it as the contribution: the contribution is the conditional-served-slice framing and the split discipline it requires, not a qualifier-stacked first-of-kind.)

## Conformal selective prediction / abstention WITH guarantees
Beyond heuristic abstention, a line of work gives *guaranteed* selective prediction. The closest neighbors are distribution-free **risk control applied to recommendation and ranking**: \citet{detoni2025flowers} use CRC to bound the expected fraction of *unwanted* items in a returned recommendation set (a per-example monotone loss controlled marginally over the full user population, with filtered items back-filled by safe ones); \citet{angelopoulos2023recsys} give a distribution-free reliability guarantee that a returned set is "mostly good" (an FDR-style / false-positive-rate control on the returned set, with a diversity objective), again over the full population; \citet{guo2023fairltr} apply distribution-free risk control post-hoc to a *stochastic* learning-to-rank model for a user-specified fairness/utility loss; \citet{xu2025twostage} control *stage-wise* retrieval losses — including nDCG-like ranking risk — for a two-stage pipeline via LTT/CRC, marginally over all queries; and conformal information retrieval returns calibrated retrieval *sets* with coverage guarantees \citep{intrator2024conformalir}. The general machinery (RCPS/CRC/LTT) is shared \citep{bates2021rcps}.

**RankCRC's difference — the distinction is the *post-abstention conditional* target, not the word "NDCG".** Because \citet{xu2025twostage} already control an nDCG-like ranking risk, "listwise NDCG" alone does *not* separate us; nor does "uses CRC", "operates on a ranker", or "label-free scores at inference" (all standard). The genuine separation is threefold and none of these neighbors has it: (i) the controlled quantity is a **conditional served-slice mean** `E[1−NDCG@k \mid \text{served}]` over the sub-population an abstention policy chose to serve — a self-normalized mean with a **random** served count `m` — whereas De Toni, Angelopoulos, Guo, and Xu all control a loss *marginally over the full population / all queries*; (ii) crucially, **a marginal all-query/stage-wise bound does not certify the served-slice mean when the served set is data-selected**: conditioning on $\{g\le\lambda\}$ creates a random-denominator winner's-curse, so the naive served estimator is *not* an honest mean of the accepted law — we repair this with the **frozen-`λ` B1/B2 split** (threshold fit on B1, losses averaged on disjoint B2) so the conditional-mean certificate is valid; (iii) the selector `g` is **label-free over rank-geometry of the base ranker's own scores** (held-out label forbidden as a feature), making the policy deployable at inference with no oracle. RankCRC thus *transfers* guaranteed-abstention machinery to recommendation with a recsys-specific **conditional** listwise target and the split discipline that conditional control requires — a protocol/application contribution, not a new conformal theorem.

## Selective prediction / classification WITHOUT guarantees
SelectiveNet \citep{geifman2019selectivenet} and the risk–coverage literature learn an abstention head trading coverage for accuracy, but provide **no distribution-free finite-sample risk guarantee** — the risk–coverage curve is empirical. **RankCRC's difference:** it adds the CRC marginal-expectation bound on top of abstention, and the selector is *label-free* over rank-geometry features rather than a jointly-trained confidence head. We include a SelectiveNet-without-guarantee baseline (trained on Fold A only, matched coverage) precisely to isolate that the *guarantee* — not merely "abstain on hard users" — is the contribution.

## LLM-uncertainty calibration
Verbalized-confidence and calibration work (ECE, temperature scaling, reliability diagrams; Kadavath et al.; Tian et al.) asks whether an LLM's stated confidence matches accuracy. **RankCRC makes no ECE / reliability-diagram claim**: the object is an operational *served-slice risk bound*, a different operational guarantee than calibration of a confidence number (different in kind — a risk bound on a served sub-population — not a strictly stronger version of ECE). Our confidence `g` is not the LLM's verbalized confidence but a logistic model over rank-geometry of the base ranker's candidate scores, with the held-out label forbidden as a feature.

## Selective / reliable recommendation and LLM-for-RecSys
Prior LLM-for-recommendation work (the 8 official baselines: ELMRec, IRLLRec, LLM2Rec, LLMEmb, LLM-ESR, ProEx, ProMax, RLMRec) targets raw-ranking improvement on the full user population. Selective / cautious recommendation has mostly been explored *heuristically* (confidence thresholds, popularity gating) without guarantees; the one distribution-free-guaranteed precedent in recommendation, \citet{detoni2025flowers}, controls the *full-population* expected fraction of unwanted items in a returned set, not a per-user-conditional ranking-quality risk on a *served slice*. **RankCRC's difference:** it reframes the problem as *risk-controlled reliability of ranking quality* — serve the slice on which the served-slice rank-risk `E[1−NDCG@k | served]` is bounded by `α` in marginal expectation — orthogonal to raw SOTA, and demonstrates (the empirical contribution, GPU-gated) that a deployable label-free-at-inference selector holds the bound across 8 domains with non-trivial coverage and transfers across domains/rankers. The sibling pointwise-posterior method (pony) owns the raw-ranking lane; TRUCE owns the reliability lane.

## Why this is not "CRC applied to NDCG"
The strongest reviewer objection is incrementality. The novelty is the *combination*: (i) listwise rank-risk as the controlled quantity; (ii) the served-slice selective framing; (iii) a **label-free, deployable** confidence over rank-geometry (no test peek), repaired with the frozen-`λ` split so the conditional-mean certificate is valid; (iv) a *falsifiable* gain law that predicts the served-slice lift a priori; and (v) the 8-domain feasibility triad (cross-domain risk control + non-trivial coverage at useful `α` + label-free confidence transfer) — which no prior conformal-recsys / conformal-ranking work \citep{detoni2025flowers,angelopoulos2023recsys,guo2023fairltr,xu2025twostage,intrator2024conformalir} demonstrates. The value is orthogonal to raw SOTA: a "serve only when the served-slice rank-risk is bounded in marginal expectation" policy is operationally useful even when raw ranking is mid-pack.

> **Citation status (citation-audit, 2026-06-20; revised after Codex GPT-5.5 xhigh positioning review):** RESOLVED for the conformal core + the recsys/ranking risk-control neighbors. `refs/conformal.bib` (11 entries, all authors/year/venue web-verified — none fabricated): Vovk–Gammerman–Shafer 2005; Angelopoulos & Bates gentle intro; Conformal Risk Control ICLR 2024; RCPS JACM 2021; Learn-then-Test 2021; Angelopoulos et al. distribution-free recsys reliability COPA 2023; Xu et al. two-stage risk control for ranked retrieval IJCAI 2025; SelectiveNet ICML 2019; **De Toni et al. "You Don't Bring Me Flowers" RecSys 2025 (CRC for unwanted recs, Best Paper); Guo et al. fair LTR with distribution-free risk control 2023; Intrator et al. conformal IR via score refinement FEVER 2024** (the three added per the review's "missing neighbors" finding). The Codex review (6.5→ addressed: refine-logs/codex_positioning_review.out) flagged (a) cherry-picked neighbor set [fixed], (b) Angelopoulos2023 mis-described as "size/coverage" → corrected to FDR/"mostly-good-set"+diversity, (c) Xu also controls nDCG-like risk so the distinction must be the post-abstention *conditional* served-slice target + frozen B1/B2 [rewritten], (d) de-stack the "to our knowledge" qualifier [made conceptual]. **Still pending (needs the experiment phase):** 8 official LLM-for-RecSys baseline citations + RankCRC empirical-claim citations, added once the results block exists; verify each the same way.

